UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 1, Lesson 2: Mapping our local area', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A map is a drawing of a place seen from above. A useful sketch map has a title, symbols explained in a key and a north arrow so a reader can follow directions.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nDraw a map from your classroom to the library with two turns. Which three things must be added so another student can use it?\n\nFEEDBACK\nA title, a key for each symbol and a north arrow. Without them a reader cannot tell what the symbols mean or which way is north.\n\nWATCH FOR\nDrawing houses from the side like a picture, or leaving out the key, gives a picture rather than a map.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nMake a map of the area within five minutes of school or a place you know well. Include eight features, a key, a north arrow and a title. Write two directions such as \"go north along ...\". Leave out your home address.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"A map is a drawing of a place seen from above. A useful sketch map has a title, symbols explained in a key and a north arrow so a reader can follow directions.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply and respond","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-GEO-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Make a map of the area within five minutes of school or a place you know well. Include eight features, a key, a north arrow and a title. Write two directions such as \"go north along ...\". Leave out your home address. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using map on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Drawing houses from the side like a picture, or leaving out the key, gives a picture rather than a map.. Correction: A title, a key for each symbol and a north arrow.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Drawing houses from the side like a picture, or leaving out the key, gives a picture rather than a map. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating map on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with map, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about map with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for map. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A title, a key for each symbol and a north arrow is correct because This matches the taught method for map. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","A title, a key for each symbol and a north arrow","Drawing houses from the side like a picture, or leaving out the key, gives a picture rather than a map","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material"],"question":"Draw a map from your classroom to the library with two turns. Which three things must be added so another student can use it?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for map; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A map is a drawing of a place seen from above","A title, a key for each symbol and a north arrow","Careful use of symbol and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Drawing houses from the side like a picture, or leaving out the key, gives a picture rather than a map"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with map?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named symbol and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply map to new material and name the symbol and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Drawing houses from the side like a picture, or leaving out the key, gives a picture rather than a map","Name symbol and explain why it matters only, without linking it to map"],"question":"Which response best applies map with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses sources, places or historical/geographic evidence appropriately for this lesson on map. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail","Drawing houses from the side like a picture, or leaving out the key, gives a picture rather than a map","A title, a key for each symbol and a north arrow","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or place detail"],"question":"Which response best uses sources, places or historical/geographic evidence for map?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a specific source or place detail and say what it shows about map","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or place detail","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail","Drawing houses from the side like a picture, or leaving out the key, gives a picture rather than a map"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about map?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (A map is a drawing of a place seen from above.) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail","Example map \"Home to the park\": a square symbol marks home, a green patch marks the park and S marks the shop","Drawing houses from the side like a picture, or leaving out the key, gives a picture rather than a map","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or place detail"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about map?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check facts, acknowledge limits of the evidence, and avoid inventing details"],"question":"Which action keeps sources, places or historical/geographic evidence accurate for map?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make a map of the area within five minutes of school or a place you know well","Retype the worked example with no new example","Drawing houses from the side like a picture, or leaving out the key, gives a picture rather than a map","Name symbol and explain why it matters only, with no linked example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies map to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about map with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating map on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using map on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for map. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with map, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Drawing houses from the side like a picture, or leaving out the key, gives a picture rather than a map.. Correction: A title, a key for each symbol and a north arrow.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y3-w01-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise map.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"History","success_criteria":["I can use map with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify symbol and explain why it matters.","I can practise key independently on a new prompt.","I can notice direction needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A map is a drawing of a place seen from above.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A map is a drawing of a place seen from above","A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the source or place"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about map with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample map \"Home to the park\": a square symbol marks home, a green patch marks the park and S marks the shop. Oak Street runs north to south and the north arrow points to the top. The key says square = home, green = park, S = shop.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDraw a map from your classroom to the library with two turns. Which three things must be added so another student can use it?\n\nFEEDBACK\nA title, a key for each symbol and a north arrow. Without them a reader cannot tell what the symbols mean or which way is north.","explain":"A map is a drawing of a place seen from above. A useful sketch map has a title, symbols explained in a key and a north arrow so a reader can follow directions.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Drawing houses from the side like a picture, or leaving out the key, gives a picture rather than a map. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"A map is a plan from above, not a side picture.","caption":"Plan view shows where things are.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Map</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">view from above</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Photo</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">side view of houses</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example map \"Home to the park\": a square symbol marks home, a green patch marks the park and S marks the shop. Oak Stree Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Example map \"Home to the park\": a square symbol marks home, a green patch marks the park and S marks the shop. Oak Stree Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","options":["A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the source or place","Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about map with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample map \"Home to the park\": a square symbol marks home, a green patch marks the park and S marks the shop. Oak Street runs north to south and the north arrow points to the top. The key says square = home, green = park, S = shop. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Key symbols and north arrow on a sketch map.","caption":"Title, key and north arrow make a map usable.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\"